:name: Flapjack CM7 SIL
:description: Single-core STM32H7 CM7 running Flapjack firmware under emulation.

# Machine setup only - no `start`, no UART backend. Callers decide how to run it
# and where the two UARTs go:
#
#   usart1  sim link   - actuators, telemetry, logs, shell
#   usart3  RX UART    - real CRSF frames from the bridge. This is the FC's only
#                        RC input; with nothing attached it never arms.
#   usart2  GPS UART   - real NMEA sentences from the bridge. Optional: with
#                        nothing attached Gps_Task simply never sees a fix.
#
# $bin is REQUIRED and deliberately has no default. The obvious one to write,
# Build/flapjack-v1/Debug/cm7.elf, is the DUAL-core image: it hard-faults on this
# platform and Renode still exits 0 having written not one UART byte, so a wrong
# path looks like a firmware bug rather than a setup mistake. Left unset it fails
# on "No such variable: $bin" instead. `board.py renode` supplies it, and checks
# the build really is SINGLE_CORE=ON first.
#
# e.g.:
#
#   # boot smoke test, log to a file
#   renode --console --disable-xwt -P -1 -e "$bin=@Build/flapjack-v1/Debug-single-core/cm7.elf; \
#       i @Scripts/renode/flapjack_sil.resc; \
#       sysbus.usart1 CreateFileBackend @boot.log; emulation RunFor \"3\"; quit"
#
#   # sim link on TCP 4000 + CRSF RC on 4001 + NMEA GPS on 4002, for bridge.py
#   # (the three sensor parts listen on 4010/4011/4012 by themselves - see the
#   # port: properties in flapjack_h7_cm7.repl)
#   renode --console -e "$bin=@Build/flapjack-v1/Debug-single-core/cm7.elf; \
#       i @Scripts/renode/flapjack_sil.resc; \
#       emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal 4000 \"simlink\" false; \
#       connector Connect sysbus.usart1 simlink; \
#       emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal 4001 \"rclink\" false; \
#       connector Connect sysbus.usart3 rclink; \
#       emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal 4002 \"gpslink\" false; \
#       connector Connect sysbus.usart2 gpslink; start"
#
# `board.py renode` wires all three for you (--port / --rc-port / --gps-port).

using sysbus
mach create "flapjack"

# Compiled at load time by Renode's built-in C# compiler. Must come before the
# platform description, which instantiates all three. Renode loads each ad-hoc
# assembly as it compiles it, so the models can reference SamplePushListener -
# but only if it is compiled first, hence the order here.
include $ORIGIN/SamplePushListener.cs
include $ORIGIN/BMI323.cs
include $ORIGIN/MMC5983.cs
include $ORIGIN/BMP390.cs

machine LoadPlatformDescription $ORIGIN/flapjack_h7_cm7.repl

# Renode's STM32H7_SPI leaves COMM and TXTFC as unimplemented tags, and
# drivers/bus/spi.c writes both on EVERY transaction - so a one-minute run buries
# the log under ~450k identical warnings (57 MB) and the interesting lines with
# it. Error level here, not silence: a real bus fault still shows.
#
# The multiplexer keeps its own level deliberately. Its "multiple devices are
# currently selected" warning is the one that catches a chip select left
# asserted, which is a fault the firmware cannot report itself.
#
# rcc spams unhandled D1/D2/D3 prescaler reads for the reason SilResearch 2
# explains - the model does not implement them, which is why the .repl pins the
# peripheral frequencies by hand.
#
# Put any of them back with e.g. `logLevel 2 sysbus.spi1` in the monitor.
logLevel 3 sysbus.spi1
logLevel 3 sysbus.spi3
logLevel 3 sysbus.spi5
logLevel 3 sysbus.rcc

# The two OnGPIO lines park the SPI5 chip selects DEASSERTED. Renode drives an
# unconfigured GPIO output low, so without them the mux starts with both parts
# selected and refuses every transfer with "multiple devices are currently
# selected" - and it never recovers, because each driver only ever raises its
# OWN NSS: the mag comes up first and is blocked by the baro's line, which
# nothing has driven yet. On the board those pins are high-Z at reset with the
# parts idle, which is what this reproduces. In the macro so it re-applies on
# `machine Reset` - SPIMultiplexer.Reset() clears its input state but not its
# selected set.
macro reset
"""
    cpu VectorTableOffset 0x08000000
    sysbus LoadELF $bin
    spi5.spi5Mux OnGPIO 0 true
    spi5.spi5Mux OnGPIO 1 true
"""
runMacro $reset
